==English==
{{was wotd|2007|September|8}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|prædilection}} {{qualifier|archaic}}

===Etymology===
{{ety|en|:bor|la-med:praedīlēctiō|text=+|tree=1}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌpɹiː.dəˈlɛk.ʃn̩/|a=UK}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-predilection.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˌpɹɛ.dəˈlɛk.ʃn̩/|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛkʃən|s=4}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A condition of [[favor]]ing or [[like|liking]]; a tendency towards; [[proclivity]]; [[predisposition]].
#* {{RQ:Landon Francesca Carrara|page=219|volume=I|text=The young King looked tenderly at Mademoiselle Mancini, who gave him a glance quite as tender in return—not, however, unobserved. His mother had been for some time past a displeased spectator of a '''predilection''' which might become dangerous.}}
#* {{RQ:Heller Catch-22|Lieutenant Scheisskopf|66|It was an illusion, of course, generated by Clevinger's '''predilection''' for staring fixedly at one side of a question and never seeing the other side at all.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1967|author=w:Flann O’Brien|title=w:The Third Policeman|chapter=2
|passage=A row of houses he regards as a row of necessary evils. The softening and degeneration of the human race he attributes to its progressive '''predilection''' for interiors and waning interest in the art of going out and staying there.}}
#* {{RQ:WaPo|date=29 Jun 1987|author=Edwin M. Yoder Jr.|title=Lewis Powell a Fine Sense of Balance
|passage=But for him the first rule of judging was to set aside personal '''predilection''' and vote the law and the facts.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=23 Oct 2000|author=Terry McCarthy|title=Lost Generation|journal=Time Asia
|passage=... youth’s '''predilection''' for revolt.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=13 Mar 2001|author=Marina Cantacuzino|title=On deadly ground|journal=The Guardian
|passage=Wilson doesn’t see any inconsistency between his socialism and his '''predilection''' for the high life.}}

